Efficiency fine-tuner (Linux / Windows, NVIDIA; root / administrator required):

The power tuner tries to reduce power consumption while keeping as much of your hashrate as possible.
It fine-tunes your existing OC (overclock settings) and chooses a result based on hashrate per watt.
The goal is better efficiency: doing more mining work for each watt of power used.
It does not find your starting core clock, memory clock or offset for you. You must choose stable starting settings first.
The feature is experimental, including Windows support (tested on Windows 10).

Before you start:

- Use a supported NVIDIA GPU and driver. Unsupported GPU/driver combinations are skipped.
  NVIDIA driver 595 or 615 is recommended.
- On Linux, run the miner as root. On Windows, run it as administrator.
- A fixed/locked core clock is required, for example through --gpu-cclock.
  GPUs using automatic/stock core clocks are skipped. Setting only --gpu-coffset is not enough to lock the core clock.
- Use one mining algorithm and one mining thread per GPU. Multiple algorithms or multiple mining threads on a GPU are not supported by the tuner.
- Keep GPU mining and OC control enabled. --gpu-disable-oc disables the power tuner.

Start with conservative OC settings:

Do not start with a very high --gpu-cclock or --gpu-coffset value. Leave some room below the GPU's stability limit.
If your current OC is already "at the edge" of stability, lower the core clock and/or core offset before enabling the tuner.
Settings that only just work without tuning can become unstable when the tuner tries to reduce power further.
First check that your starting OC mines reliably without --gpu-power-tuner, then enable it.

Results vary between individual GPUs, even with the same model and OC, because of differences in silicon quality.
Another person's clocks, offsets or tuning result may not work on your GPU.

It is recommended to set your OC through the miner's parameters and include --gpu-reset-oc.
--gpu-reset-oc resets OC to defaults on startup before the miner applies your requested settings.
This helps the miner manage GPU resets and restore your OC more reliably during recovery.
Avoid changing clocks or offsets with another tool while tuning is running.

Choose which GPUs to tune:

Use the GPU IDs shown by --list-devices. These GPUs must also be selected for mining.
--gpu-id chooses which GPUs mine; --gpu-power-tuner chooses which of those GPUs are tuned.
Selecting a GPU for tuning does not automatically select it for mining.

--gpu-power-tuner               Tune all eligible NVIDIA GPUs selected for mining.
--gpu-power-tuner 1             Tune GPU 1 only.
--gpu-power-tuner 0,2           Tune GPUs 0 and 2 only.

For the best and safest results, tune GPUs one at a time.
A crash on one GPU can affect other GPUs in the same miner process, making it harder to identify the GPU that caused the failure.

Simple starting procedure:

1. Run --list-devices and find the ID of the GPU you want to tune.
2. Set a conservative fixed core clock and, if used, core offset in your normal mining command. Check that mining is stable.
3. Add the following options to that command (this example tunes GPU 0, which must be selected for mining):

   --gpu-reset-oc --gpu-power-tuner 0 --gpu-power-tuner-loss 2.5

4. Start the miner as root/administrator and let it finish tuning. Do not keep changing the OC during the process.
5. After tuning finishes, keep mining and check stability over a longer period.

Choose how much hashrate loss is acceptable:

--gpu-power-tuner-loss sets the maximum allowed hashrate loss when choosing the tuning result.
The range is 1-25%, the default is 2.5%, and decimal values with up to two decimal places are supported. Use a dot for decimals.

For example, if your starting speed is 100 MH/s, a 2.5% limit allows a result around 97.5 MH/s or higher if it improves efficiency.
This is a limit, not a target: the tuner does not have to reduce your hashrate by that amount.

Higher values allow it to consider larger hashrate reductions for potentially greater power savings.
The limit does not prevent temporary hashrate drops or downtime while testing settings or recovering from a crash.

One value applies to every GPU selected for tuning:
--gpu-power-tuner 0,1,5 --gpu-power-tuner-loss 2
This allows up to 2% loss on each of GPUs 0, 1 and 5.

To use a different limit for each GPU, provide explicit IDs after --gpu-power-tuner and one loss value for each ID, in the same order:
--gpu-power-tuner 0,1,5 --gpu-power-tuner-loss 2,1,3.5
This allows up to 2% loss on GPU 0, 1% on GPU 1 and 3.5% on GPU 5.

The loss values follow the order of --gpu-power-tuner IDs, not the order of --gpu-id.
Do not repeat GPU IDs in a per-GPU loss list, and provide exactly as many loss values as tuner IDs.
--gpu-power-tuner-loss alone does not enable tuning; --gpu-power-tuner must also be present.

What to expect while tuning:

The miner first waits for OC setup, intensity tuning and stable measurements. Tuning may not start immediately.
Allow roughly 5-20 minutes for tuning; it can take longer if measurements need more time to settle or recovery is needed.
The miner tests settings, compares hashrate and power, and saves the result when tuning finishes.

Some GPU/OC combinations show no efficiency gain. In that case, the original settings are retained.
You can try a different stable starting OC or allow a higher loss limit, but an improvement is not guaranteed.

Multiple automatic miner restarts during tuning are normal when GPU recovery succeeds.
Tuning resumes after the restart until a sufficiently stable result is found or the original settings are restored.
On a mining OS that manages the miner process, the OS may perform the restart after the miner exits.

Some crashes leave the GPU in a state the miner cannot recover. If recovery fails, a full machine reboot is required.
If tuning repeatedly fails, reduce the starting core clock and/or core offset before trying again.
A result that passes tuning can still become unstable after extended mining; a completed tuning run is not a guarantee of long-term stability.

Saved results and later starts:

Results are saved automatically in the Autotune directory under the miner's working directory.
Keep this directory so the miner can reuse the results.

Keep --gpu-power-tuner enabled on later starts to load the saved result for the same GPU and matching mining/OC settings.

The console reports when tuning finishes and is saved, or when a saved configuration for the current OC is loaded.
Changes to OC, algorithm, intensity or loss limit can trigger fresh tuning.

If the tuner is skipped or stops, read its console message. Check privileges, GPU selection, the core clock lock and GPU/driver support first.
Also check that your requested OC was applied successfully and that the GPU is not overheating or being changed by another OC tool.
